  "worked_solution": "The derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is positive when $f(x)$ is increasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=a$ and $x=e$ are on increasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/7f42ed4bdb45d14497c70b0e2e98af95442d9bf8e6aaa577766dec1c5df2b31f.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\u2323$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\u2322$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=a$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave  down ($\u2322$) at $x=a$ only.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
